            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Introduction\r\nKangaroo Mother Care (KMC), a practice that includes skin-to-skin contact (STSC) with the mother and early initiation of breastfeeding, is recommended for preterm and/or low-birth-weight (LBW) infants. Studies have shown that in preterm and/or LBW infants, KMC is effective at reducing neonatal mortality and severe infection, and improving maternal-infant attachment. Most of these studies, however, have focused on KMC initiated in hospital settings, while the primary aim of the present study is to determine how community-initiated KMC impacts neonatal mortality. A secondary aim of this thesis is to explore potential effect modification by birth weight and gestational age. \r\n\r\nMethods\r\nThe data for this study were collected from 2004 to 2005 as part of a cluster-randomized controlled trial (RCT) in Shivgarh, a rural area in Uttar Pradesh, India. The cluster RCT included a control arm and two study arms to evaluate a package of interventions for essential newborn care, which included but was not limited to KMC. To examine the relationship between KMC and neonatal mortality, we broke the randomization provided by the cluster RCT. Poisson generalized estimation equation (GEE) modeling with robust standard errors was used to estimate the relative risk of neonatal mortality with KMC vs. no KMC, accounting for clustering by village administrative unit (the unit of allocation in the cluster RCT). Because there was a high level of missingness in KMC exposure data, a complete case analysis and a series of sensitivity analyses were performed. \r\n\r\nResults\r\nOf the 2500 participants (mother-infant dyads) included in this study, 320 (12.8%) were missing information on either STSC or immediate breastfeeding and thus were missing information on exposure to KMC. In the complete case analysis, there was a risk ratio of 0.084 (95% CI, 0.024 to 0.29, p < 0.0001), suggesting that KMC is highly effective in reducing neonatal mortality. All four sensitivity analyses yielded protective effects in the adjusted model with point estimates ranging from 0.062 to 0.35. Counts in both planned subgroup analyses (birth weight and gestational age) were too sparse to run the regression models, but neonatal deaths were higher in infants that did not receive KMC. \r\n\r\nDiscussion\r\nOverall, the results of this study suggest that community-initiated KMC is protective against neonatal mortality. However, given numerous methodologic challenges relating to data completeness and confounding, results should be interpreted cautiously. Despite being underpowered to detect effect modification by birth weight or gestational age, this study generated hypotheses that KMC may be effective in non-LBW and/or term infants.",
